  "body": {
    "id": "thm:shrinking-margin-ratio-obstruction",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "The frozen class does not determine a sharp necessary-and-sufficient shrinking-margin frontier solely from \\(m_n\\) and the sequence \\(\\eta_n(B)\\). For any joint numerator-and-denominator approximation with absolute error at most \\(\\eta_n(B)\\) and limiting cell probability at least \\(m_n>\\eta_n(B)\\), conditional-cdf error is at most \\(2\\eta_n(B)/(m_n-\\eta_n(B))\\); hence \\(\\eta_n(B)=o(m_n)\\) is a universal sufficient relation. It is not necessary as a relation involving the declared \\(\\eta_n(B)\\), because that sequence is a noncanonical upper bound that may be enlarged without changing any law or conditional error. Moreover, the frozen sampling and reference margin assumptions impose a fixed positive \\(\\epsilon\\), so no \\(m_n\\downarrow0\\) regime occurs inside \\(\\mathcal M\\). A sharp failure sequence therefore requires an enlarged class together with a canonical minimal joint-approximation modulus and explicit moving-threshold or boundary-density conditions; it cannot be constructed or characterized from the frozen primitives alone.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "B",
      "eta",
      "dsamp",
      "dref",
      "epsilon"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:sampling-selection-margin",
      "ass:reference-selection-margin",
      "thm:uniform-selected-transfer",
      "def:shrinking-margin-handle",
      "lem:conditioning-ratio-transfer"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Apply the conditioning-ratio lemma with \\(\\delta=\\eta_n(B)\\) and \\(m=m_n\\).  It gives conditional-cdf error at most\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\eta_n(B)}{m_n-\\eta_n(B)},\n\\]\nwhich vanishes under \\(\\eta_n(B)=o(m_n)\\).  If the limiting density near the critical quantile is bounded below by \\(g_n>0\\), the quantile error is at most \\(2\\eta_n(B)/\\{g_n(m_n-\\eta_n(B))\\}\\); threshold bracketing then transfers power.\n\nNecessity cannot be expressed through the declared remainder.  If \\(\\eta_n(B)\\) is valid, any decreasing \\(\\widetilde\\eta_n(B)\\ge\\eta_n(B)\\) tending to zero is also valid, although its ratio to \\(m_n\\) can differ.  Absolute numerator and denominator errors may also cancel in their ratio.  Finally, the two frozen margin assumptions bound every represented cell probability below by the fixed \\(\\epsilon>0\\), so \\(m_n\\downarrow0\\) does not occur within \\(\\mathcal M\\).  A sharp frontier would require an enlarged class, a canonical minimal modulus, and moving-boundary conditions; none is supplied by the frozen primitives.",
